		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>A cleaning company usually offers more reliability and peace of mind: insured, vetted staff, cover when your cleaner is ill, and a business to go back to if something isn't right. A self-employed cleaner can offer a more personal and flexible arrangement, but you rely on one person and have to check their insurance and background yourself.</p>
<p>Neither choice is automatically better. Plenty of households in Hull and East Yorkshire are happy with either, and the right answer depends on what you value most: continuity, flexibility, cover or cost.</p>
<p>We're a cleaning company, so we'll be upfront about that, and we've tried to give a fair picture of both sides below.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>A company can usually send cover for sickness and holidays; a sole trader often can't.</li><li>Check insurance and DBS status either way, as neither is guaranteed with any cleaner.</li><li>Independent cleaners can be flexible and personal, but you rely on one person.</li><li>If a cleaner works only for you on your terms, be aware of employment status rules.</li><li>Whoever you choose, agree the price and the task list in writing.</li></ul></div>
<h2>Cleaning company vs self-employed cleaner at a glance</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Cleaning company</th><th>Self-employed cleaner</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Business insurance usually covers every cleaner sent</td><td>Varies, so ask to see their own policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vetting</td><td>Company carries out DBS checks and recruitment</td><td>Up to you to check DBS and references</td></tr>
