</tbody>
</table>
<p>The sections below explain each point in more detail, starting with what a company brings.</p>
<h2>Pros of using a cleaning company</h2>
<h3>Cover when your cleaner is off</h3>
<p>Illness, holidays and family emergencies happen. A company can usually send another cleaner, so your home doesn't go without for a fortnight. For busy households and anyone who relies on a fixed routine, this is often the deciding factor.</p>
<h3>Vetting and insurance taken care of</h3>
<p>A company handles recruitment, DBS checks and insurance for everyone it sends. You should still ask to see proof, but you aren't relying on a single person's paperwork. Our <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular house cleaning</a> is carried out by insured, DBS-checked and vetted cleaners.</p>
<h3>Accountability</h3>
<p>If a clean falls short, there's an office to call and a process for putting it right. We offer a re-clean if something isn't right, which takes the awkwardness out of raising a problem.</p>
<h3>More services under one roof</h3>
<p>The same company can often handle a <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a>, oven cleaning or <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet and upholstery cleaning</a> with the right equipment, so you don't need to find a different trader for each job.</p>
<h2>Cons of using a cleaning company</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Less personal</strong> – you're dealing with a business as well as a person, and some people simply prefer a one-to-one arrangement.</li>
<li><strong>Your cleaner may change</strong> – staff move on, and even well-run companies can't promise the same face forever.</li>
<li><strong>Set procedures</strong> – companies work to agreed task lists and policies, so ad hoc requests outside the agreed scope may need sorting out with the office first.</li>
<li><strong>Cost</strong> – insurance, vetting, training, equipment and admin are all overheads, which can mean a company costs more than a sole trader. Compare what's included, not just the headline price.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pros of hiring a self-employed cleaner</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A personal relationship</strong> – the same person every time, who gets to know your home and how you like things done.</li>
<li><strong>Flexibility</strong> – many independents will swap tasks around from week to week or fit in small extras.</li>
<li><strong>Direct communication</strong> – you agree everything with the person doing the work, with no office in between.</li>
<li><strong>Supporting a local sole trader</strong> – many good independent cleaners work in smaller communities such as <a href="{{area:driffield}}">Driffield</a> and <a href="{{area:pocklington}}">Pocklington</a>, often finding customers by word of mouth.</li>
<li><strong>Potentially lower cost</strong> – with fewer overheads, some independents charge less, though this isn't always the case.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Cons of hiring a self-employed cleaner</h2>
<h3>No cover for sickness and holidays</h3>
<p>If your cleaner is ill or away, the visit usually doesn't happen. That's fine for some households and a real problem for others, particularly if you work long hours or have a busy family home.</p>
<h3>You do the checks</h3>
<p>Insurance and DBS checks aren't legal requirements for a self-employed domestic cleaner, so you need to ask for both and check the documents yourself. Our guide on <a href="{{guide:how-to-choose-a-cleaner}}">how to choose a cleaner you can trust</a> walks through what to look for.</p>
<h3>Less recourse if things go wrong</h3>
<p>With one person, there's nobody to escalate to. If you fall out, or something is damaged and they aren't insured, sorting it out can be awkward for both of you.</p>
<h3>Employment status</h3>
<p>Most self-employed cleaners work for several clients and choose their own methods, so they're genuinely self-employed. But if a cleaner works only for you, on hours and terms you dictate, HMRC could treat them as your employee, which brings responsibilities such as tax and National Insurance. If your arrangement looks like that, check the official guidance on employment status.</p>
<h2>Which is right for your home?</h2>
<p>A cleaning company tends to suit you if:</p>
<ul>
<li>You need cleans to happen on schedule, even when someone is off.</li>
<li>You'll be handing over a key and want a formal key holding process, as explained in our guide to <a href="{{guide:giving-your-cleaner-a-key}}">giving your cleaner a key</a>.</li>
<li>You'd like one provider for regular, deep and specialist cleaning.</li>
<li>You want vetting, insurance and complaints handled by a business.</li>
</ul>
<p>An independent cleaner may suit you better if:</p>
<ul>
<li>You value a one-to-one relationship above everything else.</li>
<li>Your needs change week to week and you want lots of flexibility.</li>
<li>You're comfortable checking insurance and references yourself and can manage without sickness cover.</li>
</ul>
<p>Whichever route you take, agree a clear list of tasks. Our breakdown of <a href="{{guide:what-does-a-regular-cleaner-do}}">what a regular cleaner does</a> and our <a href="{{guide:weekly-cleaning-schedule}}">realistic weekly cleaning schedule</a> are useful starting points. It's also worth running through our <a href="{{guide:questions-to-ask-a-cleaning-company}}">questions to ask a cleaning company</a>, as most of them work just as well for an independent cleaner.</p>
<h2>Thinking about a cleaning company?</h2>
<p>If a company sounds like the better fit, our team provides <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">weekly and fortnightly house cleaning</a> across <a href="{{area:hull}}">Hull</a> and East Yorkshire. Our cleaners are insured, DBS-checked and vetted, bring their own equipment and products, and work to a fixed quote agreed before the first visit.</p>
<p>If you'd like to talk it through before deciding, <a href="{{page:contact}}">get in touch with our team</a> and we'll give you a straight answer.</p>
