	[
		'slug'        => 'preparing-for-your-first-clean',
		'title'       => 'Preparing for Your First Professional Clean',
		'seo_title'   => 'Preparing for Your First Professional Clean: Checklist',
		'description' => 'Preparing for your first professional clean: what to tidy, what to tell your cleaner, and how to sort keys, pets and parking so the first visit goes smoothly.',
		'category'    => 'hiring-a-cleaner',
		'service'     => 'one-off-cleaning',
		'related'     => [ 'what-does-a-regular-cleaner-do', 'giving-your-cleaner-a-key', 'deep-clean-vs-regular-clean' ],
		'image'       => 'tidy kitchen worktop',
		'excerpt'     => 'What to tidy, what to tell your cleaner and how to sort access, pets and parking so your first professional clean goes smoothly.',
		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>To prepare for your first professional clean, clear clutter from surfaces and floors, put away valuables and anything fragile, sort out access and parking, and tell your cleaner your priorities and anything that's off-limits. You don't need to clean before the cleaner arrives, but a quick tidy means more of their time goes on actual cleaning.</p>
<p>A little preparation the night before makes a noticeable difference to the result. Here's what to do, based on what our team sees on first visits to homes across Hull and East Yorkshire.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Tidy, don't clean: clear clutter so cleaners can reach surfaces and floors.</li><li>Tell your cleaner what matters most and what to avoid.</li><li>Sort out keys, parking, alarms and pets before the day.</li><li>Put away valuables, medication and private paperwork.</li><li>Walk round afterwards and give feedback while it's fresh.</li></ul></div>
<h2>Should you tidy before a cleaner comes?</h2>
<p>Yes, but tidy rather than clean. Cleaners are there to clean, and the time they spend picking up toys, folding clothes or clearing the kitchen table is time not spent on the skirting boards and bathroom.</p>
<p>There's no need to feel embarrassed about the state of your home. Our cleaners have seen it all, and a house that needs serious attention is exactly why people book a <a href="{{service:one-off-cleaning}}">one-off clean</a>. Preparation is simply about letting them get on with the job.</p>
<h2>What to do before your first clean: a checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Clear worktops and tables</strong> of post, toys, chargers and dishes so surfaces can be wiped properly.</li>
<li><strong>Pick things up from the floor</strong>, including clothes, shoes, bags and cables, so floors can be hoovered and mopped edge to edge.</li>
<li><strong>Load or empty the dishwasher</strong> and leave the sink clear.</li>
<li><strong>Put away valuables</strong>, cash, jewellery, medication and private paperwork.</li>
<li><strong>Move fragile ornaments</strong> you'd rather not have handled.</li>
<li><strong>Strip the beds</strong> if you'd like them changed, and leave clean linen out.</li>
<li><strong>Check the hot water is on</strong> so your cleaner has what they need.</li>
<li><strong>Clear access</strong> to the areas you most want cleaned, such as the bath, the hob or the space behind the sofa.</li>
</ol>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Leave a short note listing your top three priorities. If time runs short on the day, your cleaner will know exactly where to focus.</p></div>
<h2>Tell your cleaner your priorities and what's off-limits</h2>
<p>The first clean is when your cleaner learns your home. The more they know, the better the result.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Priorities</strong> – the limescale on the shower screen, the greasy extractor hood, the hallway carpet.</li>
<li><strong>Special surfaces</strong> – natural stone, marble, oiled wood worktops, unsealed floors and high-gloss units all need specific products.</li>
<li><strong>Off-limits areas</strong> – a home office, a teenager's bedroom or a cupboard you'd rather keep closed.</li>
<li><strong>Known problems</strong> – black mould, damp patches, old stains or a loose tile.</li>
</ul>
<p>Hard water is worth mentioning too. Much of Hull and East Yorkshire has hard water, so limescale builds up quickly on taps and glass, and our <a href="{{guide:hard-water-limescale-east-yorkshire}}">hard water and limescale guide</a> explains why. If your home needs more than a general once-over, a <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> may suit you better. Our guide to the <a href="{{guide:deep-clean-vs-regular-clean}}">difference between a deep clean and a regular clean</a> will help you decide.</p>
<h2>Access, keys, parking and alarms</h2>
<p>Practical details cause more first-visit hiccups than anything else, so sort them in advance.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Getting in</strong> – will you be home, or leaving a key? If you won't be in, read our guide to <a href="{{guide:giving-your-cleaner-a-key}}">giving your cleaner a key safely</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Alarms</strong> – explain how the alarm works, or turn it off if you're there to let them in.</li>
<li><strong>Parking</strong> – our cleaners bring their own equipment, so a space nearby helps. On narrow terraced streets or in residents' permit zones, tell us the best place to park.</li>
<li><strong>Rubbish</strong> – say where bin bags and recycling should go, especially if bin day falls on cleaning day.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pets and children on cleaning day</h2>
<p>Pets can be curious, nervous or determined to supervise. If you can, keep dogs in a closed room or take them out with you, and let us know about any cats so doors aren't left open. Mention pet hair hotspots too; our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-with-pets}}">cleaning a home with pets</a> covers what helps between visits.</p>
<p>If children are home, it's easier for everyone if they're kept away from floors being mopped and rooms where products are in use.</p>
<h2>What happens on the day of your first clean</h2>
<p>Your cleaner arrives with equipment and products, including eco-friendly options if you've asked for them. If you're in, they'll have a quick walk round with you to confirm priorities. Most cleaners work from top to bottom and room by room, finishing with the floors.</p>
<p>You don't need to stay. Plenty of customers head to work and come back to a finished home. If you're staying in, working from one room while the cleaner does the rest works well.</p>
<p>A first clean usually takes longer than later visits, because the cleaner is learning your home and there's often some build-up to tackle.</p>
<h2>After the clean: check and give feedback</h2>
<p>Walk round soon afterwards and look at the areas you flagged. If anything has been missed or isn't right, tell us promptly and we'll put it right with a re-clean. Feedback also shapes future visits, so if you'd like more time spent on the kitchen and less on the spare room, say so.</p>
<p>If you're still comparing options, our guide on <a href="{{guide:how-to-choose-a-cleaner}}">how to choose a cleaner you can trust</a> covers insurance, DBS checks and reviews.</p>
<h2>Booking your first clean with us</h2>
<p>A <a href="{{service:one-off-cleaning}}">one-off clean from our team</a> is a good way to get your home back on track, and many customers move on to <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular house cleaning</a> afterwards. We cover <a href="{{area:hull}}">Hull</a> and East Yorkshire, including <a href="{{area:anlaby-willerby-kirk-ella}}">Anlaby, Willerby and Kirk Ella</a>, with a fixed quote agreed before work starts.</p>
<p>To arrange your first clean or ask what to expect, <a href="{{page:contact}}">get in touch with our team</a>.</p>
