	[
		'slug'        => 'what-does-a-regular-cleaner-do',
		'title'       => 'What Does a Regular Cleaner Do? (And What They Don’t)',
		'seo_title'   => 'What Does a Regular Cleaner Do? A Room-by-Room Guide',
		'description' => 'What does a regular cleaner do? A room-by-room guide to what’s included in a regular clean, what’s usually extra, and how often to book visits.',
		'category'    => 'hiring-a-cleaner',
		'service'     => 'regular-house-cleaning',
		'related'     => [ 'deep-clean-vs-regular-clean', 'weekly-cleaning-schedule', 'preparing-for-your-first-clean' ],
		'image'       => 'vacuum cleaner living room',
		'excerpt'     => 'A room-by-room look at what a regular cleaner does on each visit, what’s usually extra, and how to get the most from your cleaner.',
		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>A regular cleaner keeps your home clean week to week: dusting, hoovering and mopping floors, cleaning kitchen worktops, the sink and the outside of appliances, and cleaning the bathroom, including the toilet, basin, bath and shower. What they don't usually do is deep, specialist or heavy work, such as cleaning inside the oven, shampooing carpets or moving heavy furniture.</p>
<p>Exact lists vary between cleaners, so it's worth agreeing yours in writing. Below is what a typical regular clean covers in homes across Hull and East Yorkshire, what's usually extra, and how to get the most out of each visit.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Regular cleaning maintains a home; it isn't designed to fix months of build-up.</li><li>Kitchens, bathrooms, floors and dusting are the core of every visit.</li><li>Inside ovens, fridges and cupboards, plus carpet shampooing, are usually extras or separate services.</li><li>Most people choose weekly or fortnightly visits.</li><li>Agree a written task list and rotate extra jobs between visits.</li></ul></div>
<h2>What does a regular cleaner do on each visit?</h2>
<p>Our <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular house cleaning</a> follows a consistent routine, which is broadly what you should expect from any regular cleaner. Here's a room-by-room view.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Room</th><th>Usually included</th><th>Usually extra or separate</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Kitchen</td><td>Worktops, splashbacks, sink and taps, hob top, outside of appliances and cupboard doors, floor</td><td>Inside the oven, inside the fridge or freezer, inside cupboards, extractor filters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bathroom</td><td>Toilet, basin, bath, shower and screen, taps, mirrors, tiles within reach, floor</td><td>Heavy limescale removal, deep grout cleaning, black mould treatment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Living areas</td><td>Dusting surfaces, skirting boards within reach, hoovering, mopping hard floors, straightening cushions</td><td>Carpet or upholstery shampooing, high-level dusting that needs ladders</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bedrooms</td><td>Dusting, hoovering, making or changing beds if clean linen is left out</td><td>Inside wardrobes and drawers, mattress cleaning</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hall and stairs</td><td>Hoovering stairs and landing, dusting banisters, wiping light switches and door handles</td><td>Washing walls, cleaning light fittings</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Most cleaners work from top to bottom, dusting before hoovering and finishing with hard floors, so dust isn't knocked onto surfaces that have already been cleaned.</p>
<h2>Tasks a regular cleaner can often do if you ask</h2>
<ul>
<li>Changing beds, if clean linen is left out</li>
<li>Emptying bins and sorting recycling</li>
<li>Wiping out the microwave</li>
<li>Cleaning interior windows and sills within reach</li>
<li>Loading or unloading the dishwasher</li>
<li>Rotating jobs, such as one room's skirting boards each visit or the inside of the fridge once a month</li>
</ul>
<p>Laundry and ironing vary a lot between cleaners and companies, so ask directly rather than assuming. Many people find that a <a href="{{guide:weekly-cleaning-schedule}}">realistic weekly cleaning schedule</a> helps them decide what the cleaner covers and what the household keeps on top of between visits.</p>
<h2>What a regular cleaner doesn't usually do</h2>
<h3>Deep and specialist cleaning</h3>
<p>Regular visits are about maintenance. Heavy grease, thick limescale or years of build-up need more time and different products than a routine visit allows. That's the job of a <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a>, which many people book once before regular visits begin. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:deep-clean-vs-regular-clean}}">deep cleaning vs regular cleaning</a> explains the difference, and our advice on <a href="{{guide:how-often-should-you-deep-clean}}">how often you should deep clean</a> helps you plan it.</p>
<h3>Ovens and carpets</h3>
<p>Cleaning inside an oven properly takes time and the right products. We offer fume-free, non-caustic <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">oven cleaning</a> as a separate service. Carpets and rugs are hoovered on regular visits but not shampooed; that needs hot water extraction equipment and is booked separately.</p>
<h3>Heavy, hazardous or unsafe work</h3>
<ul>
<li>Moving heavy furniture or appliances</li>
<li>Working at height on ladders</li>
<li>Outside windows above ground level</li>
<li>Biohazards, such as large amounts of bodily fluids or pest droppings</li>
<li>Gardening, clearing rubbish or sorting out lofts and garages</li>
</ul>
<h3>Personal tidying and sorting</h3>
<p>Cleaners clean around your belongings. Most won't sort through paperwork, fold piles of clothes or decide what to throw away, and they shouldn't open private drawers unless you ask them to.</p>
<h2>How often should a regular cleaner come?</h2>
<p>Weekly visits suit busy families, homes with pets and anyone who likes things consistently tidy. Fortnightly works well for smaller homes, couples and people who keep on top of daily tidying themselves. Monthly visits are possible, but by then dust and limescale have built up, so each visit becomes closer to a mini deep clean.</p>
<p>Homes with dogs or cats usually benefit from weekly visits, because hair and muddy paw prints build up fast; see our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-with-pets}}">cleaning a home with pets</a>. Water is the other local factor. Much of East Yorkshire has hard water, so taps and shower screens need regular attention to <a href="{{guide:hard-water-limescale-east-yorkshire}}">stop limescale building up</a>.</p>
<h2>How long does a regular clean take?</h2>
<p>It depends on the size of your home, the number of bathrooms, pets and what's on your task list. A one-bedroom flat in the Old Town or at Victoria Dock needs far less time than a four-bedroom family home in Kingswood or Sutton. We'll suggest a sensible visit length when we quote and keep it consistent from week to week.</p>
<p>The first visit usually takes longer, as the cleaner learns the layout and tackles any build-up. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:preparing-for-your-first-clean}}">preparing for your first professional clean</a> helps that visit go smoothly.</p>
<h2>Getting the most from your regular cleaner</h2>
<ul>
<li>Agree a written task list and update it when your needs change.</li>
<li>Tidy surfaces and floors before visits so time goes on cleaning, not clearing.</li>
<li>Leave a note for anything urgent or different that week.</li>
<li>Ask for a rotation of extra jobs so the whole house gets attention over a month.</li>
<li>Give feedback early, both positive and negative.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Regular house cleaning in Hull and East Yorkshire</h2>
<p>Our team provides <a href="{{local:regular-house-cleaning-hull}}">regular house cleaning in Hull</a> and in towns including <a href="{{area:hedon}}">Hedon</a> and <a href="{{area:hessle}}">Hessle</a>. Our cleaners are insured and DBS-checked, bring their own equipment and products, and work to a fixed quote agreed before the first visit.</p>
<p>If you'd like help deciding what your clean should include, find out more about our <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">weekly and fortnightly cleaning service</a> or <a href="{{page:contact}}">contact our team</a> and we'll talk it through.</p>
